REPORTED FAULT: For the call `temp3({...})` in `testIssue700`, argument type checking computes the actual argument type wrongly and emits `JSC_TYPE_MISMATCH. actual parameter 1 of temp3 does not match formal parameter found   : {b: boolean, n: number, text: (string|undefined)} required: (undefined|{b: boolean, n: number, text: string})`. The wrong value is the inferred actual type `{b: boolean, n: number, text: (string|undefined)}` for that object literal.

ROOT CAUSE: `matchConstraint` applies record constraints to any `PrototypeObjectType`, even though the comment says it should only do so for anonymous types. That lets non-anonymous object types be mutated by structural matching, which can inject inferred `undefined|T` properties and later contaminate argument type checking.

PATCH EFFECT: The patch stops `matchConstraint` only when `this` is a native object type and the constraint is a record type; in practice here, it prevents mutating the native `Object` type. It does not stop the same wrong computation for other non-anonymous, non-native `PrototypeObjectType` instances.

SURVIVING INPUT: A concrete surviving case is:
```js
/** @constructor */ function Foo() {}
/** @param {{text:string}} x */ function temp1(x) { return x.text; }
/** @param {Foo} x */ function trigger(x) {
  /** @notypecheck */ function inner() { return temp1(x); }
  return inner();
}
/** @param {{text:string}} x */ function use(x) {}
function callee() { use(new Foo()); }
```
`matchConstraint` still runs on the named, non-native `Foo` instance type, infers `text` as `(string|undefined)`, and can still produce the same kind of wrong actual-parameter mismatch at `use(new Foo())`.

SIBLING PATHS: No separate sibling function in the listed reachable region is needed: the residue is still in the patched `PrototypeObjectType.matchConstraint` itself for non-native named types. A concrete call is `fooInstanceType.matchConstraint(recordTypeForTextString)`.

DECISION: The patch fixes the reported test by blocking one manifestation on native `Object`, but it does not remove the underlying bug that `matchConstraint` still mutates other non-anonymous object types.

