REPORTED FAULT:  
For the call `temp3({...})`, the type-checker computes the argument type incorrectly at actual-parameter checking and reports `found   : {b: boolean, n: number, text: (string|undefined)} required: (undefined|{b: boolean, n: number, text: string})`. The wrong value is that `text` is returned as `(string|undefined)` instead of `string` in that call check.

ROOT CAUSE:  
`matchConstraint` mutates any `PrototypeObjectType` that is matched against a record constraint, even though the method comment says it should only match constraints on anonymous types. That lets shared, non-anonymous object types pick up inferred optional properties like `text: (string|undefined)`, which then contaminates later type checks.

PATCH EFFECT:  
The patch adds an early return only when `this` is a native object type and the constraint is a record type. So it prevents mutation of native shared types like the built-in `Object`, but still allows the same mutation on other non-anonymous `PrototypeObjectType`s.

SURVIVING INPUT:  
```js
/** @constructor */ function Foo() {}
/** @param {{text: string}} x */ function temp1(x) {}
/** @param {{activity: (Foo|number)}} opt_data */
function temp2(opt_data) {
  /** @notypecheck */ function __inner() { temp1(opt_data.activity); }
  __inner();
}
/** @param {{text: string}} x */ function temp3(x) {}
function callee() {
  var x = new Foo();
  x.text = 'a';
  temp2({activity: x});
  temp3(x);
}
```  
Under the patch, `Foo` is not native, so `matchConstraint` still mutates it and `temp3(x)` can still be checked with `text: (string|undefined)`, producing the same kind of wrong actual-parameter mismatch.

SIBLING PATHS:  
No separate sibling path is needed: the unpatched computation survives in the same concrete call `FooInstanceType.matchConstraint(recordType)` inside `PrototypeObjectType.matchConstraint`. I do not see another function in the listed reachable region that independently reproduces this exact reported fault once this method is correct.

DECISION:  
The patch fixes the reported test by special-casing native object types, but it does not remove the underlying bug that non-anonymous prototype object types are still mutated by record constraints.

