REPORTED FAULT: For the call to `temp3`, the type checker computes the actual argument type incorrectly and reports `found   : {b: boolean, n: number, text: (string|undefined)} required: (undefined|{b: boolean, n: number, text: string})`. The wrong value is the inferred actual parameter type at that call site: `text` is made `(string|undefined)` when the test expects no warning.

ROOT CAUSE: `matchConstraint` applies record constraints to any `PrototypeObjectType`, even though the comment says it should only match constraints on anonymous types. That wrongly mutates reusable object types by defining inferred optional properties (`void ∪ propType`) on types that should not be rewritten.

PATCH EFFECT: The patch skips this mutation only when `this` is a native object type and the constraint is a record type. So it prevents one source of pollution, but it still rewrites non-native named `PrototypeObjectType`s.

SURVIVING INPUT: A concrete survivor is a named constructor instance type:
```js
/** @constructor */ function Foo() { this.n = 0; this.b = true; }
/** @param {{text:string}} x */ function temp1(x) { return x.text; }
/** @param {Foo} x */ function temp2(x) { /** @notypecheck */ function inner() { return temp1(x); } return inner(); }
/** @param {{n:number,text:string,b:boolean}} x */ function temp3(x) { return ''; }
function callee() { var x = new Foo(); x.text = 'a string'; temp3(x); }
```
`temp2` still drives `matchConstraint` on non-native `Foo`, so `text` is inferred as `(string|undefined)` on `Foo`, and the later call to `temp3(x)` can still produce the same kind of actual-parameter mismatch.

SIBLING PATHS: No separate sibling function is needed; the unpatched computation remains in the same `PrototypeObjectType.matchConstraint` call for non-native named object types such as `new Foo()`. I do not see another distinct reachable function that reproduces the reported fault independently of this method.

DECISION: The patch fixes the reported test by blocking one manifestation on native objects, but it does not remove the underlying cause that `matchConstraint` still rewrites non-anonymous non-native object types.

